-- title:
--   Cube membership as a maximum-norm bound
-- statement:
--   For a real side length $h$ and points $u, x \in \mathbb{R}^d$, membership of $x$ in the cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ is characterised by
--   $$x \in A_h(u) \iff \|x - u\|_\infty < h/2 ,$$
--   where $\|w\|_\infty = \max_{1 \le i \le d} |w_i|$ is the maximum norm of the coordinates. That is, the cube is precisely the open ball of radius $h/2$ about $u$ in the maximum norm.
--
--   This identification is what lets cubes be handled by norm inequalities rather than coordinatewise case analysis: the comparison between the maximum norm and the Euclidean norm then transfers immediately into a comparison between cubes and Euclidean balls, which is how a cube of side $h$ is shown to sit inside a Euclidean ball of radius $h\sqrt{d}/2$ about the same centre.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis is placed on $h$; for $h \le 0$ the right-hand side is unsatisfiable and the cube is the empty set.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Cubes.lean#L98-L99

